 questions: [
  ["On Scammon's growth curves, lymphoid tissue is unusual because it:","Exceeds adult size in late childhood and then regresses",["Reaches adult size earliest of all tissues, by about age 6","Grows mainly after puberty, alongside the genital tissues","Follows the same curve as the face"],"Tonsils and adenoids overshoot before shrinking, which is why airway obstruction peaks in childhood; neural tissue is the earliest to reach adult size."],
  ["Growth of the mandible most closely follows which of Scammon's curves?","General (somatic)",["Neural (cranial vault)","Lymphoid (tonsils)","Genital (reproductive)"],"The mandible tracks the general body curve with a pubertal spurt, while the maxilla sits further toward the neural curve; the cranial vault follows the neural curve and is nearly complete early in childhood."],
  ["Which of the following is a true growth centre with independent growth potential?","Spheno-occipital synchondrosis",["Mandibular condylar cartilage","Zygomaticomaxillary suture","Coronal suture"],"Cranial base synchondroses are primary cartilages that grow when transplanted; the condylar secondary cartilage and the sutures grow in response to their surroundings and are classed as sites."],
  ["The anterior surface of the maxilla during childhood growth is predominantly:","A resorptive surface, while the maxilla is displaced forward",["A depository surface whose apposition pushes the face forward","Inactive until puberty","The main site of sutural growth"],"Enlow showed the maxilla moves forward by displacement from cranial base and sutural growth while its anterior surface remodels by resorption; the apparent contradiction is the classic examination point."],
  ["The mandibular condyle grows in which direction?","Upward and backward",["Downward and forward","Upward and forward","Directly downward"],"Condylar proliferation is up and back; because the condyle is held in the glenoid fossa, the mandible as a whole is displaced down and forward."],
  ["Lengthening of the mandibular body to accommodate the permanent molars occurs mainly by:","Posterior ramus deposition with anterior ramus resorption",["Deposition on the chin and lower border of the body","Growth at the mental symphysis throughout childhood","Apposition on the anterior ramus with posterior ramus resorption"],"The ramus relocates backward by posterior apposition and anterior resorption, converting ramus into body; the symphysis fuses in infancy and the chin barely grows."],
  ["Compared with boys, the adolescent growth spurt in girls occurs:","About two years earlier",["About two years later","At the same age","Only after menarche"],"Girls mature earlier, peaking around 12 versus 14 in boys; menarche typically follows the peak, so it signals that most of the spurt has passed."],
  ["A 9-year-old boy has a skeletal Class III malocclusion with maxillary deficiency. The most appropriate orthopaedic approach is:","Facemask (reverse-pull headgear) protraction now",["Functional appliance to advance the mandible at 14","Orthognathic maxillary advancement surgery at 12","Chin cup to restrain the mandible at 17"],"Maxillary protraction works best early, before the circummaxillary sutures mature; surgery must wait until mandibular growth has finished, which is late in boys, and advancing the mandible would worsen a Class III."],
  ["The functional matrix theory of Moss proposes that:","Bone grows in response to its surrounding soft tissues and spaces",["Sutures are the primary determinants of facial growth, pushing bones apart","The nasal septum cartilage pushes the maxilla forward","Growth is genetically determined within bone and its cartilages"],"Moss argued the brain, eyes, airway and muscles are the drivers and bone follows; sutural dominance (Sicher) and nasal septum (Scott) theories are the alternatives offered as distractors."],
  ["Late crowding of the lower incisors in the late teens is best explained by:","Continued mandibular growth and rotation after maxillary growth stops",["Eruption of the maxillary canines into a crowded upper arch","An increase in lower intercanine width after the permanent canines erupt","Loss of leeway space in the mixed dentition"],"Late mandibular growth and rotation push the lower incisors against the stable upper arch; third molars are no longer considered the main cause, and lower intercanine width decreases rather than increases in this period."],
  ["A 7-year-old sustained a condylar fracture that healed with ankylosis. The most likely long-term consequence is:","Mandibular asymmetry with deficient growth on the affected side",["Mandibular asymmetry with excessive growth on the affected side","No effect, since the condyle is a growth centre","Maxillary overgrowth on the affected side"],"Ankylosis stops the condyle translating and restricts growth on that side, so the affected side falls behind, with deviation of the chin toward that side."],
  ["Among the following, the most useful method of judging how much pubertal growth remains in an orthodontic patient is:","Cervical vertebral maturation on the lateral cephalogram",["Chronological age compared with population averages","Dental age assessed from root development on a panoramic radiograph","Counting erupted permanent teeth"],"Skeletal maturity, judged on vertebral shape (or a hand-wrist film), predicts the spurt better than age or tooth eruption, which vary widely between individuals."],
  ["During growth, the hard palate descends because of:","Resorption on its nasal surface and deposition on its oral surface",["Deposition on its nasal surface and resorption on its oral surface","Growth at the midpalatal suture, widening the arch","Elongation of the nasal septum"],"Remodelling moves the palate downward while the midpalatal suture widens the arch; the oral surface is depository despite the palate moving down."],
  ["The maxilla is carried downward and forward mainly by:","Cranial base displacement and growth at its cranial sutures",["Endochondral growth of a primary maxillary cartilage","Apposition on its anterior surface pushing it forward","Eruption of the permanent molars"],"The maxilla has no primary cartilage; it is pushed by the growing cranial base and grows at the frontomaxillary, zygomaticomaxillary, zygomaticotemporal and pterygopalatine sutures."],
  ["A 15-year-old boy with a skeletal Class III due to mandibular prognathism asks for orthognathic surgery. The most appropriate response is to:","Delay surgery until serial records confirm growth has ceased",["Operate now, while bone healing is fastest at this age","Fit a functional appliance to redirect mandibular growth","Extract the lower first premolars as definitive treatment"],"Mandibular growth in boys can continue into the late teens; operating early risks relapse as the mandible keeps growing. Functional appliances cannot restrain a prognathic mandible at this age."]
 ]
